{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"modifying-algorithms","title":"Transforming & Modifying Algorithms","group":"STL Algorithms & Ranges","order":58,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Algorithms that write: copy, transform, fill, replace, reverse, rotate — and the remove-erase idiom, the most misunderstood algorithm pair in the STL.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Copy, transform, fill</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;cctype&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; src{1, 2, 3, 4};\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; dst(src.size());\n    std::transform(src.begin(), src.end(), dst.begin(), [](int x) { return x * x; });\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; sums;\n    std::transform(src.begin(), src.end(), dst.begin(), std::back_inserter(sums), std::plus&lt;&gt;{});   // binary\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; big;\n    std::copy_if(dst.begin(), dst.end(), std::back_inserter(big), [](int x) { return x &gt; 4; });\n\n    std::string s = \"Hello\";\n    std::transform(s.begin(), s.end(), s.begin(), [](unsigned char c) { return std::toupper(c); });\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; filled(4);\n    std::fill(filled.begin(), filled.end(), 7);\n    std::fill_n(filled.begin(), 2, 0);\n\n    for (auto* vec : {&amp;dst, &amp;sums, &amp;big, &amp;filled}) {\n        for (int x : *vec) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"| \";\n    }\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 4 9 16 | 2 6 12 20 | 9 16 | 0 0 7 7 | HELLO</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Output algorithms don't grow the destination. <code>std::copy(a.begin(), a.end(), empty.begin())</code> writes past the end of an empty vector — use <code>std::back_inserter(empty)</code> or size the destination first.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>The remove–erase idiom</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 0, 2, 0, 3, 0};\n    auto newEnd = std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 0);   // shifts keepers forward; size unchanged!\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; v.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (newEnd - v.begin()) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    v.erase(newEnd, v.end());                           // actually shrink\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; w{5, 6, 7, 8, 9};\n    w.erase(std::remove_if(w.begin(), w.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2; }), w.end());\n    std::erase(w, 8);                                   // C++20: both steps in one call\n    for (int x : w) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 3\n123\n6</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Why two steps?</div><p><code>std::remove</code> can't change a container's size — it only sees iterators. It moves the elements to keep to the front and returns the new logical end; <code>erase</code> removes the leftover tail. In C++20, <code>std::erase</code> / <code>std::erase_if</code> do both.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Reorder: reverse, rotate, unique, shuffle</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;random&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nvoid show(const std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; v) { for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x; std::cout &lt;&lt; ' '; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    std::reverse(v.begin(), v.end());              show(v);\n    std::rotate(v.begin(), v.begin() + 2, v.end()); show(v);   // element at +2 becomes first\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; d{1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 1};\n    d.erase(std::unique(d.begin(), d.end()), d.end());          // collapse *adjacent* duplicates\n    show(d);\n\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; r{1, 2, 3};\n    std::replace(r.begin(), r.end(), 2, 9);                      show(r);\n    std::swap(r[0], r[2]);                                       show(r);\n\n    std::mt19937 rng(42);\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; deck{1, 2, 3, 4, 5};\n    std::shuffle(deck.begin(), deck.end(), rng);\n    std::sort(deck.begin(), deck.end());                         show(deck);   // back in order\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>54321 32154 1231 193 391 12345</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Dedupe a whole vector</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v{3, 1, 3, 2, 1};\n    std::sort(v.begin(), v.end());                        // 1 1 2 3 3\n    v.erase(std::unique(v.begin(), v.end()), v.end());    // 1 2 3\n    for (int x : v) std::cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; ' ';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 3</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does std::unique do?</summary><div><p>It removes <em>consecutive</em> duplicate elements by shifting the unique ones forward and returning the new logical end (like <code>remove</code>). To dedupe an arbitrary vector, sort it first, then <code>erase(unique(...), end())</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}